{"name":"Creamy Oat","hex":"#ffe0af","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"creamy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing cream.","Of food or drink, having the rich taste or thick, smooth texture of cream, whether or not it actually contains cream."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A horse with a cream-coloured coat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/creamy"},{"term":"oat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Widely cultivated cereal grass, typically Avena sativa.","Any of the numerous species, varieties, or cultivars of any of several similar grain plants in genus Avena."]},{"part":"Prepositional phrase","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of OAT."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Creamy","blocks":[{"text":"Creamy were a Danish-Faroese teen-pop duo, composed of Rebekka Mathew and Rannva Joensen."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creamy","wikidata":"Q5183392"},{"title":"Oat","blocks":[{"text":"The oat, sometimes called the common oat, is a species of cereal grass (Avena) grown for fodder and for its seed, which is known by the same name. Oats appear to have been domesticated as a secondary crop, as their seeds resembled those of other cereals closely enough for them to be included by early cultivators. Oats tolerate cold winters less well than cereals such as wheat, barley, and rye, but need less summer heat and more rain, making them important in areas such as Northwest Europe that have cool, wet summers. They can tolerate low-nutrient and acid soils. Oats grow thickly and vigorously, allowing them to outcompete many weeds, and compared to other cereals are relatively free from diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/AvenaSativa3.jpg/330px-AvenaSativa3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oat","wikidata":"Q12104"}],"images":[{"s